<section class="purchase-permissions">
    <h3 class="learn-h">@L["Purchase routes and permissions"]</h3>
    <p class="learn-lead">@L["Where a buyer finds an offer is not necessarily where they pay. The offer's available purchase routes come first; an M365 licence alone does not choose the route."]</p>
    <div class="permission-table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="@L["Purchase routes and permissions"]">
        <table>
            <caption>@L["Illustrative requirements — this demo does not check accounts, cards, roles or tenant policies."]</caption>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">@L["Purchase route"]</th>
                    <th scope="col">@L["What the buyer needs"]</th>
                    <th scope="col">@L["Where the customer organization controls it"]</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row">@L["Web checkout with a card"]</th>
                    <td>@L["Work or school account and a valid card. The published Web purchase requirements do not require an Entra admin role in advance."]</td>
                    <td>@L["Third-party SaaS self-service purchase policy (AllowSelfServicePurchase / OfferType SaaS), plus sign-in restrictions."]</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row">@L["Using an existing company MCA billing profile"]</th>
                    <td>@L["Permission to use that profile in a purchase, such as Billing profile Contributor or Owner. Not a requirement for every first-time card purchase."]</td>
                    <td>@L["Microsoft 365 admin center → Billing accounts → Billing profiles → Billing profile roles."]</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row">@L["Azure checkout — reached from the Web or the portal"]</th>
                    <td>@L["Eligible Azure subscription and Azure RBAC permissions for the purchase. Being able to view the subscription is not enough."]</td>
                    <td>@L["Azure IAM and Marketplace purchase controls, including Private Marketplace approvals. The Web SaaS self-service policy does not block Azure portal purchases."]</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row">@L["After purchase: the publisher landing page"]</th>
                    <td>@L["Entra sign-in and any required app consent; link the purchase to the intended user or company account."]</td>
                    <td>@L["The customer's Entra policies govern sign-in. The publisher's backend grants access from the contract, not from an email domain alone."]</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p class="learn-foot">@L["MCA billing-profile roles are not MOSA billing-administrator roles. Billing administration, self-service purchasing and permission to use the SaaS are separate."]</p>
    <p class="learn-foot">@L["OfferType SaaS controls third-party SaaS as a type; it is not a per-vendor allow-list. Azure Private Marketplace can approve specific offers and plans. Publishers cannot override a customer's purchase restrictions."]</p>

    <h4>@L["One landing page, more than one arrival"]</h4>
    <p class="learn-lead">@L["Buyers can configure immediately after ordering, later from a confirmation email or order history, or return to manage an active subscription. The existing active-subscription check handles the return without another activation."]</p>
    <p class="learn-foot">@L["For the manual-activation flow illustrated here, complete configuration within 30 days. This is guidance, not a timer implemented by this demo; auto-activated plans follow a different flow."]</p>
    <p class="learn-foot">@L["A Windows app can open the offer in a browser, then use its existing backend to check access. The SaaS backend being on Azure does not by itself give the buyer Azure purchase rights."]</p>
